---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import GovernedTransactionLoop from "../components/diagrams/GovernedTransactionLoop.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How It Works | BeaconGuard Governed AI Transaction"
  description="See how BeaconGuard governs the release, authorizes provider invocation, treats AI results as untrusted proposals, authorizes the declared enterprise action, and records decisions for verification."
>
  <section class="stack-section">
    <h1>How It Works</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A governed AI transaction is a closed cycle: the enterprise application
      prepares a request, may invoke a provider after admission ALLOW, receives
      an untrusted result, declares an intended action, and executes only after
      independent action ALLOW—returning to the same application trust boundary.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Complete governed cycle</h2>
    <p>
      The diagram communicates the full loop before the numbered stages. Provider
      success is not enterprise authorization.
    </p>
    <GovernedTransactionLoop
      variant="detailed"
      title="Detailed governed transaction loop"
      caption="Six lifecycle stages mapped onto one closed transaction that returns to the enterprise application."
    />
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>1. Govern the release</h2>
    <p>
      Compliance Playbooks are validated, approved, signed, distributed, and
      activated. Only an Active Verified Release governs later authorization
      decisions. Distribution alone does not activate a release.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>2. Authorize the request</h2>
    <p>
      Request Admission Authorization decides whether the enterprise application
      may invoke the AI provider under the Active Verified Release. Outcomes are
      binary: <strong>ALLOW</strong> or <strong>DENY</strong>. DENY records the
      decision and blocks provider invocation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>3. Invoke the provider</h2>
    <p>
      Only after ALLOW may the enterprise application invoke the AI provider for
      that governed request. BeaconGuard does not become the model host or
      replace the customer's chosen provider.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>4. Receive an untrusted result</h2>
    <p>
      The AI-generated result—including any tool or action proposal—is an
      untrusted proposal. It does not confer enterprise authority. Using the AI
      response itself as authorization is not supported.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>5. Authorize the intended action</h2>
    <p>
      The enterprise application declares the intended enterprise action.
      Result-to-Action Authorization is independent authorization of that
      declared action under the Active Verified Release—not semantic scoring of
      model content. DENY prevents enterprise effect. ALLOW permits the
      application to execute the declared action.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>6. Record and verify</h2>
    <p>
      Authorization decisions produce Verifiable Decision Records. BeaconGuard
      Verify supports investigation, reconstruction, replay verification,
      reporting, and export. Verify is read-only and cannot grant runtime
      authority or mutate a historical DENY.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack-section">
    <h2>Fail closed</h2>
    <p>
      The request or declared action is blocked when authorization cannot
      complete safely. Review may produce obligations for a later request; it
      does not convert a recorded DENY into ALLOW or resume an original provider
      response as authority.
    </p>
    <div class="page-actions">
      <a class="btn-primary" href="/canonical-workflow">Canonical Workflow</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/platform">Platform map</a>
      <a class="btn-secondary" href="/verification">Verification</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
